1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,600
August 2nd, 216 BC. Southern Italy.

2
00:00:05,200 --> 00:00:09,300
The Roman Republic has just done
something it has never done before.

3
00:00:09,380 --> 00:00:12,350
It has raised the biggest
army in its entire history.

4
00:00:12,400 --> 00:00:15,000
Eighty-six thousand men.

5
00:00:15,200 --> 00:00:19,550
And across the field? A Carthaginian
general, with about fifty thousand…

6
00:00:19,600 --> 00:00:22,300
…and one working eye.

7
00:00:22,600 --> 00:00:25,950
By sunset, the Roman
army won't be defeated.

8
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:27,250
It won't retreat.

9
00:00:27,300 --> 00:00:29,300
It won't surrender.

10
00:00:29,400 --> 00:00:33,000
It will simply… cease to exist.

11
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:41,750
To understand why, you have to
know about Rome and Carthage.

12
00:00:41,800 --> 00:00:46,230
Two superpowers fighting over
the western Mediterranean.

13
00:00:46,280 --> 00:00:52,970
One is an empire of farmers and
soldiers — stubborn, organized, brutal.

14
00:00:53,020 --> 00:00:58,590
The other is a trading empire, wealthy,
with the best navy in the world and enough

15
00:00:58,640 --> 00:01:01,910
money to hire any mercenary they need.

16
00:01:01,960 --> 00:01:03,030
They already fought once.

17
00:01:03,080 --> 00:01:06,090
Twenty-three years of war.

18
00:01:06,140 --> 00:01:08,110
Hundreds of thousands dead.

19
00:01:08,160 --> 00:01:09,830
And Rome won.

20
00:01:09,880 --> 00:01:13,890
But one Carthaginian general
didn't handle losing well.

21
00:01:13,940 --> 00:01:16,550
His name was Hamilcar Barca.

22
00:01:16,600 --> 00:01:21,210
And he made a promise to his son
at an altar — a promise so strong,

23
00:01:21,260 --> 00:01:23,210
it would change history.

24
00:01:23,260 --> 00:01:25,590
That boy was named Hannibal.

25
00:01:25,640 --> 00:01:29,550
And Hamilcar told him: 'Never
be at peace with Rome.'

26
00:01:29,600 --> 00:01:32,700
Hannibal never forgot.

27
00:01:32,750 --> 00:01:35,500
Years later, Hannibal is a general.

28
00:01:35,550 --> 00:01:37,280
War breaks out again.

29
00:01:37,330 --> 00:01:41,500
Rome expects to fight
in Spain, maybe Africa.

30
00:01:41,550 --> 00:01:42,780
Normal places.

31
00:01:42,830 --> 00:01:45,040
Hannibal has a different idea.

32
00:01:45,090 --> 00:01:47,020
He's going to march overland.

33
00:01:47,070 --> 00:01:48,520
Through the Pyrenees.

34
00:01:48,570 --> 00:01:50,120
Across southern France.

35
00:01:50,170 --> 00:01:51,200
Over the Alps.

36
00:01:51,250 --> 00:01:52,140
In autumn.

37
00:01:52,190 --> 00:01:53,820
With war elephants.

38
00:01:53,870 --> 00:01:55,360
It was insane.

39
00:01:55,410 --> 00:01:58,320
He lost half his men on the way.

40
00:01:58,370 --> 00:02:00,620
Almost all the elephants died.

41
00:02:00,670 --> 00:02:01,830
Soldiers froze.

42
00:02:01,880 --> 00:02:04,440
Rockslides buried whole units.

43
00:02:04,490 --> 00:02:05,760
But in the end...

44
00:02:05,810 --> 00:02:08,800
...a Carthaginian army
appeared in northern Italy.

45
00:02:08,850 --> 00:02:10,300
Out of nowhere.

46
00:02:10,350 --> 00:02:12,450
Rome didn't see it coming.

47
00:02:12,500 --> 00:02:15,590
So Rome sent an army to stop him.

48
00:02:15,640 --> 00:02:19,710
At the River Trebia, December 218 BC.

49
00:02:19,760 --> 00:02:24,750
Hannibal hid his entire army and waited
for the Romans to wade through an icy river

50
00:02:24,800 --> 00:02:26,850
before breakfast.

51
00:02:26,900 --> 00:02:31,950
By the time they realized they were being
attacked, they were already surrounded.

52
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:35,070
Thousands drowned trying to escape.

53
00:02:35,120 --> 00:02:37,930
Rome tried again the next year.

54
00:02:37,980 --> 00:02:41,470
Lake Trasimene, June 217 BC.

55
00:02:41,520 --> 00:02:43,830
Hannibal does the same thing.

56
00:02:43,880 --> 00:02:48,490
He hides his army in the
hills above a foggy lakeshore.

57
00:02:48,540 --> 00:02:51,040
The Romans march straight into the trap.

58
00:02:51,090 --> 00:02:54,340
Fifteen thousand Romans
die in a few hours.

59
00:02:54,390 --> 00:02:57,290
Fifteen thousand more are captured.

60
00:02:57,340 --> 00:02:59,510
Their general is killed.

61
00:02:59,560 --> 00:03:04,490
And somewhere during all this, Hannibal
marched through a swamp for days.

62
00:03:04,540 --> 00:03:05,950
He got an infection.

63
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:08,110
Lost the sight in one eye.

64
00:03:08,160 --> 00:03:09,750
But did he go home?

65
00:03:09,800 --> 00:03:10,850
No.

66
00:03:10,900 --> 00:03:11,210
He just...

67
00:03:11,260 --> 00:03:15,658
put on an eyepatch and kept going.

68
00:03:15,708 --> 00:03:19,898
After two catastrophic defeats,
Rome did something smart.

69
00:03:19,948 --> 00:03:24,878
They gave power to an old senator
named Quintus Fabius Maximus.

70
00:03:24,928 --> 00:03:28,478
And Fabius had one strategy: don't fight.

71
00:03:28,528 --> 00:03:32,618
Instead, he would follow
Hannibal around Italy.

72
00:03:32,808 --> 00:03:34,138
Cut off his supplies.

73
00:03:34,188 --> 00:03:35,698
Pick off small groups.

74
00:03:35,748 --> 00:03:38,218
But never engage in a big battle.

75
00:03:38,268 --> 00:03:43,528
Hannibal was in enemy territory,
far from home, running out of food.

76
00:03:43,578 --> 00:03:45,558
Time was on Rome's side.

77
00:03:45,608 --> 00:03:46,958
It was working.

78
00:03:47,008 --> 00:03:49,818
But the Romans absolutely hated it.

79
00:03:49,868 --> 00:03:52,998
They gave Fabius a nickname: 'Cunctator.'

80
00:03:53,048 --> 00:03:54,918
It means 'The Delayer.'

81
00:03:54,968 --> 00:03:56,558
It was not a compliment.

82
00:03:56,608 --> 00:04:01,458
So in 216 BC, Rome elects two new consuls.

83
00:04:01,508 --> 00:04:04,958
Lucius Aemilius Paullus
and Gaius Terentius Varro.

84
00:04:05,008 --> 00:04:08,158
And they decide: no more waiting.

85
00:04:08,208 --> 00:04:11,098
They raise the biggest
army Rome has ever raised,

86
00:04:11,148 --> 00:04:13,938
and they're going to find
Hannibal and crush him.

87
00:04:13,988 --> 00:04:15,078
Today.

88
00:04:15,128 --> 00:04:17,158
One of them wants to be cautious.

89
00:04:17,208 --> 00:04:19,408
The other wants blood.

90
00:04:19,458 --> 00:04:24,508
The two armies meet near a small town
called Cannae, in southern Italy.

91
00:04:24,558 --> 00:04:27,728
There's a river, the Aufidus, on one side.

92
00:04:27,778 --> 00:04:30,248
Open plains everywhere else.

93
00:04:30,298 --> 00:04:34,308
Today, the consuls take turns commanding.

94
00:04:34,358 --> 00:04:35,768
One day each.

95
00:04:35,818 --> 00:04:39,828
Today is Varro's turn — the impatient one.

96
00:04:39,998 --> 00:04:42,368
Varro's strategy is simple.

97
00:04:42,418 --> 00:04:44,568
He has twice as many soldiers.

98
00:04:44,618 --> 00:04:49,468
He's going to pack them into one
massive block — shoulder to shoulder,

99
00:04:49,518 --> 00:04:55,748
rank after rank — and push straight through
Hannibal's center like a battering ram.

100
00:04:55,798 --> 00:04:59,028
Hannibal looks at this and smiles.

101
00:04:59,078 --> 00:05:01,588
Because he's already won.

102
00:05:01,638 --> 00:05:06,628
He puts his weakest troops — Gauls
and Spaniards — in the center.

103
00:05:06,678 --> 00:05:11,948
And he makes them form a crescent
shape, bulging out toward the Romans.

104
00:05:11,998 --> 00:05:19,008
Behind them, out of sight, he places his
best soldiers: hardened African infantry.

105
00:05:19,058 --> 00:05:23,148
On the wings, his cavalry —
heavy horsemen on one side,

106
00:05:23,198 --> 00:05:26,068
fast Numidian riders on the other.

107
00:05:26,118 --> 00:05:27,648
And one more thing.

108
00:05:27,698 --> 00:05:30,648
The wind that day is hot and dusty.

109
00:05:30,698 --> 00:05:33,908
It's blowing directly
into the Romans' faces.

110
00:05:33,958 --> 00:05:35,568
The battle starts.

111
00:05:35,618 --> 00:05:41,288
The Roman block charges into Hannibal's
center, and at first, it's working.

112
00:05:41,338 --> 00:05:43,928
The Gauls and Spaniards are pushed back.

113
00:05:43,978 --> 00:05:45,648
Step by step.

114
00:05:45,698 --> 00:05:47,308
The crescent flattens.

115
00:05:47,358 --> 00:05:49,968
Then it caves inward.

116
00:05:50,018 --> 00:05:51,728
The Romans are winning.

117
00:05:51,778 --> 00:05:53,148
They can feel it.

118
00:05:53,198 --> 00:05:56,828
They push deeper and deeper into the gap.

119
00:05:56,878 --> 00:05:58,968
More men crowd in behind them.

120
00:05:59,018 --> 00:06:02,028
And then Hannibal says one word: 'Now.'

121
00:06:02,078 --> 00:06:08,668
On both sides, the African soldiers turn
inward and slam into the Roman flanks with

122
00:06:08,718 --> 00:06:10,128
their spears.

123
00:06:10,178 --> 00:06:16,068
At the same time, Hannibal's heavy cavalry
have already crushed the Roman horsemen

124
00:06:16,118 --> 00:06:17,508
by the river.

125
00:06:17,558 --> 00:06:19,768
But they don't stop to celebrate.

126
00:06:19,818 --> 00:06:21,748
They don't chase the runners.

127
00:06:21,798 --> 00:06:26,048
They ride all the way around,
behind the Roman army.

128
00:06:26,098 --> 00:06:30,388
They help the Numidians drive
off the last Roman cavalry units.

129
00:06:30,438 --> 00:06:33,448
Then they charge straight
into the Roman rear.

130
00:06:33,498 --> 00:06:36,308
The Roman army is completely surrounded.

131
00:06:36,358 --> 00:06:40,228
So tightly packed they can
barely raise their swords.

132
00:06:40,278 --> 00:06:43,428
The men in the front don't
know what's happening.

133
00:06:43,478 --> 00:06:46,048
The men in the back can't escape.

134
00:06:46,098 --> 00:06:49,908
They're crushed together
like sardines in a can.

135
00:06:49,958 --> 00:06:53,018
By sunset, it was over.

136
00:06:53,078 --> 00:06:57,328
Polybius says around seventy
thousand Romans died that day.

137
00:06:57,378 --> 00:07:01,048
Other historians say forty-eight thousand.

138
00:07:01,098 --> 00:07:07,068
Either way, it was one of the bloodiest
single days of battle in all of history.

139
00:07:07,118 --> 00:07:09,378
The consul Paullus died on the field.

140
00:07:09,428 --> 00:07:16,548
Varro escaped — running away on horseback,
his overconfidence now a liability.

141
00:07:16,658 --> 00:07:20,828
Hannibal lost somewhere between
six and eight thousand men.

142
00:07:21,042 --> 00:07:25,412
Hannibal's brother sailed
home to Carthage with news.

143
00:07:25,462 --> 00:07:30,752
He walked into the senate chamber and
poured out a pile of gold rings onto the

144
00:07:30,802 --> 00:07:32,072
floor.

145
00:07:32,122 --> 00:07:36,092
Rings taken from the fingers
of dead Roman nobles.

146
00:07:36,142 --> 00:07:37,512
Thousands of them.

147
00:07:37,562 --> 00:07:40,572
Everyone in Rome expected surrender.

148
00:07:40,622 --> 00:07:45,112
One of Hannibal's cavalry commanders,
Maharbal, begged him to march on Rome

149
00:07:45,162 --> 00:07:46,892
immediately.

150
00:07:46,942 --> 00:07:50,412
'You know how to win a
victory,' Maharbal said.

151
00:07:50,462 --> 00:07:52,972
'But you don't know how to use one.'

152
00:07:53,022 --> 00:07:54,712
Hannibal hesitated.

153
00:07:54,762 --> 00:07:57,292
And that hesitation changed everything.

154
00:07:57,342 --> 00:07:58,192
Because Rome...

155
00:07:58,242 --> 00:08:01,072
simply refused to give up.

156
00:08:01,122 --> 00:08:02,352
No peace talks.

157
00:08:02,402 --> 00:08:03,872
No surrender.

158
00:08:03,922 --> 00:08:06,152
They raised new legions.

159
00:08:06,202 --> 00:08:08,192
They even armed slaves.

160
00:08:08,242 --> 00:08:13,572
And they went back to the strategy
everyone had hated: Fabius's strategy.

161
00:08:13,622 --> 00:08:15,312
The Delayer's strategy.

162
00:08:15,362 --> 00:08:19,032
Hannibal stayed in Italy
for another thirteen years.

163
00:08:19,082 --> 00:08:21,352
He won every battle he fought.

164
00:08:21,402 --> 00:08:22,952
But he never took Rome.

165
00:08:23,002 --> 00:08:25,252
Never even got close.

166
00:08:25,302 --> 00:08:31,192
In the end, Rome took the war to Africa,
and Hannibal was finally defeated at Zama

167
00:08:31,242 --> 00:08:33,382
in 202 BC.

168
00:08:34,222 --> 00:08:36,112
But Cannae was never forgotten.

169
00:08:36,162 --> 00:08:41,712
For more than two thousand years,
military academies have studied it.

170
00:08:41,762 --> 00:08:47,752
Modern generals still use it as the perfect
example of surrounding and destroying a

171
00:08:47,802 --> 00:08:49,272
bigger enemy.

172
00:08:49,322 --> 00:08:53,612
Hannibal won the most important
battle of the century.

173
00:08:53,662 --> 00:08:55,908
And he still lost the war.

174
00:08:55,958 --> 00:08:57,548
So what do we learn from Cannae?

175
00:08:57,598 --> 00:09:00,868
That numbers don't always win.

176
00:09:00,918 --> 00:09:04,568
That strategy beats brute force.

177
00:09:04,698 --> 00:09:10,088
That one general's genius can matter
more than eighty-six thousand soldiers.

178
00:09:10,138 --> 00:09:11,488
But also...

179
00:09:11,538 --> 00:09:14,128
that even genius has limits.

180
00:09:14,178 --> 00:09:16,328
Even Hannibal couldn't break Rome.

181
00:09:16,378 --> 00:09:19,768
Because Rome was more
than one city or one army.

182
00:09:19,818 --> 00:09:21,208
It was an idea.

183
00:09:21,258 --> 00:09:24,388
And ideas are harder to kill than men.

184
00:09:24,438 --> 00:09:26,448
Next time: Kadesh.

185
00:09:26,498 --> 00:09:31,088
Where two pharaohs both claimed
they won, carved it in stone,

186
00:09:31,138 --> 00:09:33,908
and we still don't know who was right.

187
00:09:33,958 --> 00:09:37,450
Subscribe to Spears &
Tears, so you don't miss it.

